{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Settled the boundary judgment I had left unresolved by reading the contract context instead of parking it. The observation still stands — the leader's real heartbeat set follower 2's reported leader to 1 (index 56) and the same type with no term field from node 3 was accepted with no error and moved it to 3 (index 57) — so the predicate remains contradicted, but the defect claim is now refuted on three sourced grounds: Step is documented on both interfaces only as advancing the state machine with a given message, with no duty to validate the term field (node.go 155-156); doc.go documents that MsgProp is deliberately term-less because the send path overwrites its term, showing an unset term is a marker for local or forwarded traffic rather than an error signal (doc.go 306-312); and the path needs a sender emitting a network type with that field unset, which no correct peer does, so no crash-fault schedule reaches it. I recorded the counterargument honestly — a hardened implementation could check membership and term, the snapshot just does not require it — and kept the consequence bounded: the follower's forwarded proposals are misdirected and its timer reset, the log self-heals on the next genuine leader message, and the main finding is untouched because its message needs no illegal field and its duty is stated by the module's own inbound filter. Validation passed with no execution requested.\"}"}}
